note right of record_event
  Spec decisions enforced here:

  D1: the row rides the caller's own
  transaction -- record_event never
  opens or commits its own transaction,
  so the event and the state change it
  describes commit or roll back
  together, atomically.

  D2: a system actor (worker,
  moderation pipeline) carries
  actor_id=None; a user actor
  (HTTP caller) always carries one.
  Enforced twice: Actor.__post_init__
  (construction-time) and the
  ck_pipeline_event_system_actor_null
  DB CHECK (backstop for any non-ORM
  writer).

  D3: payload is PII-free by contract.
  _PAYLOAD_ALLOWLIST is a closed
  per-event-type key set; values are
  restricted to None/bool/int/float/a
  bounded (<=200 char) string, or a
  str->int count mapping. Free text
  (story prose, a child's name, the
  reviewer's send-back prose) is
  structurally rejected, never merely
  discouraged.

  Note what SENT_BACK does and does not
  carry, since the two are easy to
  conflate. Its payload key reason_code
  is a value from publishing/reason_codes.py's
  CLOSED vocabulary, D3-compliant by
  construction and persisted as the review
  scorecard's structured label. The
  reviewer's free-text reason travels in
  send_back()'s separate argument, is
  logged, and never reaches this payload.
end note

note bottom of pipeline_event_table
  Bold arrows mark the three workflows
  this diagram was scoped to trace
  (generation, moderation, publishing);
  the remaining call sites (story
  requests, threshold admin, assignments
  (+ unassign, G8), ratings, the WS-J admin
  surfaces (user management, families,
  family connections), #270's kid flags,
  passage editor, admin re-screen, Phase 8a's
  admin profile-view audit, WS-8's cell
  saturation signal, ADR-023 P3/P4
  personalization + ring-2 consent, the
  moderation-review-redesign remoderation
  endpoint, the generation status endpoint
  and RQ failure path (api/generation.py,
  generation/queue.py), and self-service
  account changes (api/me.py)) complete the
  42-site total enumerated in this file's
  header. Re-derive that total with:
  grep -rn "record_event(" --include=*.py src
  (excluding events/writer.py's definition).
  Downstream readers: the admin
  moderation dashboard and the guardian
  notification feed (notifications/ package)
  both project pipeline_event rows read-only;
  neither ever writes one.
end note

@enduml
